Owned-account container audit

Confirmed source defects fixed
- Virtual getPackageUid replaced installed guest package names with the host package, returning the physical UID while ApplicationInfo exposed a virtual UID.
- Unknown virtual UIDs were attributed to the calling package. Unknown UIDs now return no packages.
- AccountManager forwarded the physical Binder UID and app-op host package to the authenticator. It now derives the virtual UID/package from the engine's process record and retains the real PID.
- Native Binder identity reused the process-start calling UID. It now resolves each current caller PID in the same virtual profile. Android provider/AttributionSource framework checks retain the physical UID.
- The SDK can send a physical UID hint. The runtime treats that as a transport alias only; package and PID validation still use the resolved caller and a different virtual UID is rejected.
- Account persistence failures were reported as successful account creation. The atomic-write result is now propagated and a newly created in-memory account is removed after failure.
- Account enumeration included an unfiltered path. Visibility rules now apply to it, and cached tokens are not returned by the generic cache path without readable visibility.
- Runtime metadata/option aliases could change the upstream client's package/certificate. Owned-client tests disable these aliases and reject identity-alias metadata.
- Authentication logs printed account bundles, OAuth tokens, response objects, key data and exception messages. The audited authentication paths now use fixed stages, enums, numeric HTTP status or redacted objects.
- Malformed auth response lines indexed a nonexistent field. Parsing now fails with a generic error and never echoes response content.
- Device check-in ran only after account-token exchange. When the user enabled check-in and ID inclusion and an ID is missing, registration now precedes token exchange with a bounded deadline. Disabled check-in remains disabled.

Not established
The generic "There was a problem communicating with Google servers" message does not identify the server's rejection. These local source defects are confirmed; their causal contribution to the reported server error has NOT been reproduced on a device. DNS, TLS, check-in, master-token exchange, account-token exchange, server-policy/DroidGuard rejection, registration/certificate mismatch and account visibility remain distinguishable stages rather than an asserted backend diagnosis.

Verification boundary
No owned test APK/source, its Google OAuth configuration, or verified registered package/certificate was supplied. No usable Android device/emulator was available. Thus no actual Google account authentication, owned-client OAuth/Play Games login, virtual service binding on-device, or successful owned-app rendering has been claimed. The default loader blocks test-app import/launch until explicit owned-client configuration is supplied.

Identity/certificate limitations
The runtime uses com.google.android.gms and the Companion uses com.android.vending. The actual APK certificates differ between the source-built development runtime and official microG Companion. The existing engine compatibility view applies only to exact hash-pinned replacement-service APKs; it does not change or re-sign the owned test APK, grant OS privileged permissions, provide Google's private key, or guarantee Google accepts the private account protocol. A genuinely registered Android OAuth client requires the owned app's actual package and SHA-1 signing certificate; a compatible server/web client ID may also be required by that app's flow. The loader's SHA-256 pin checks the actual installed client signer. The registration declaration is input, not independent proof of Google Cloud registration. MicroG account login is also not proof of Play Games/cloud-save API support.

Scope
New host package com.dynamongamer.ownedaccountprobe. UI actions: Set up account, Launch test app, Diagnostics. Both account APKs bundled and verified before import. Virtual enabled services/providers and the com.google authenticator are audited at setup. Only an explicitly configured owned APK is imported unchanged with base/split byte checks and real-certificate validation. No third-party game has been modified, injected into, re-signed, launched, stopped or had its data changed by this new loader. Passwords, cookies, OAuth and refresh tokens are not diagnostic payloads.

Tests and builds
17 standalone regression suites passed (policyChecks). Gradle :Bcore:testDebugUnitTest and :app:testDebugUnitTest completed as NO-SOURCE. Host :app:assembleDebug and runtime :play-services-core:assembleDefaultRelease succeeded. Host and runtime APK signatures verified. CRC, bundled runtime/Companion bytes and source hash pins matched. ARM64 package. Actual Google login/owned-client launch test: BLOCKED; no owned client configuration/device.

